hash.c: an entry walk carries an index, not a moving pointer (4.0.0-rc2).

Preparation for sub-letting the hash's entry array, which is the nesting level above
the GC's object slots: measured on this pin, three defects in that array go unseen by
revoke-on-free, because a slot is vacated by ar_delete/ht_delete and refilled by a
later store with nothing released (capstone/bug-corpora/mruby/gc-slot-repros).

Giving each slot its own capability is impossible while a walk increments a pointer
from slot i to slot i+1, so both walks now carry an INDEX and take their entry from
ea_get(), which is already the one accessor that turns an index into an entry. The
same holds for entry_skip_deleted(), which gains an index form beside it. Slot birth
and slot death were ALREADY funnelled -- ea_set() is the only function that fills a
slot and entry_delete() the only one that vacates it -- so this is the last piece
before sublet_take and sublet_give have one site each.

Both walks stayed SINGLE loops deliberately. The obvious index form nests a second
for inside the first, and five of the nine walk bodies use break or continue with two
using both, so nesting would change their control flow silently -- and mrbtest would
not necessarily show it, since it need not reach every break. A single loop keeps
break and continue bound exactly where they were. The cost is that entry_var is now
the caller's variable rather than the macro's, which adds one declaration at each of
the 22 call sites (8 EA_EACH/EA_EACH_USED, 14 H_EACH).

Behaviour is unchanged and was checked rather than assumed: with this patch mrbtest is
Total 1527, OK 1527, KO 0, Crash 0, Skip 0, and the same tree with the pin's own
hash.c is Total 1527, OK 1527, KO 0, Crash 0, Skip 0 -- byte-identical counts. The
seven hash cases in the corpus also return their previous verdicts exactly, which
covers the delete-during-callback paths the walk macros are reached through.

In this build ea_get() still returns &ea[index], so the seven places that take an
entry's index back as `entry - ea` keep working. Under a sub-let they will not, and
that is the next patch's business, not this one's.

